   "en": "Multiple real tailwinds, but the addressable slices Hyarks can actually win are narrower than the headline. (1) TUNA/FISHING: the global purse-seine tuna fleet (~6,600 vessels, ~27% of fleet) is fuel-intensive (~368 L fuel per tonne landed; global fishing fleet burned ~31B liters in 2023 per ScienceDirect), and ~90% of tuna is caught by ~2% of vessels using helicopters — a concrete, fuel-heavy pain point the TF-100 directly targets. The tuna-fishing-vessel market is ~US$1.5-2.4B (some sources cite up to US$17B incl. catch value). This is Hyarks' most defensible, near-term wedge and the source of the \"fuel-saving\" narrative. (2) OFFSHORE WIND: real GSEA relevance (Taiwan is a leading Asian offshore-wind market off Changhua), inspection/O&M is a growing USV use case. (3) DEFENSE: the biggest TAM but most contested — Taiwan has a ~NT$1.25T (~US$39.8B) special defense budget targeting 1,000-1,600+ unmanned attack vessels, and the broader autonomous-ships market is ~US$9B (2025). Timing is excellent on the defense tailwind, but procurement flows to incumbents (see competitors). Overall: strong macro timing, but the realistic SAM for a sub-10-person startup is the fishing/offshore-wind dual-use niche, not the marquee defense numbers."
